WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.960
General chat models provide broad assistance. Coding models emphasize software tasks.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.190
Reasoning-oriented models may spend more time on complex problems.

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:11.160
Embedding models convert text into vectors for retrieval.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.075
Vision-language models process images as well as text.

00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:17.960
Select models by task tests, language needs, context, license, provenance,

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.700
runtime support, memory, latency, and error behavior.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.690
A larger model may improve difficult work while making ordinary interaction slow or

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.480
expensive.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.775
Quantization reduces numerical precision to lower memory and compute requirements.

00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:31.850
Quality tradeoffs vary by model and task.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.695
Compare several practical quantizations instead of assuming the highest precision is

00:00:35.695 --> 00:00:40.229
always the best system choice. Ollama and LM Studio simplify common local workflows.

00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:43.960
llama.cpp provides lower-level control for compatible models.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.880
Open WebUI offers a web interface that can connect to supported services.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.560
API-compatible servers help applications integrate with local inference.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.840
Runtime choice affects model formats, drivers, acceleration, context, batching,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.360
concurrency, logging, APIs, and security.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.440
A model that works in one tool may behave differently in another because prompt templates

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.960
and generation defaults change.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.440
Shortlist a few complete configurations and evaluate them before committing.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.200
Record model, file, quantization, runtime, backend, prompt template, settings, hardware,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.395
and date. Treat model upgrades as application changes, not transparent replacements.

00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:20.950
A model-routing or fallback policy can assign simple work to a small local model and

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.080
escalate difficult requests to an approved stronger service or human.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.550
Routing must honor data classification and user expectations.
